Hoarding Disorder
Mental Health & Substance Use
Difficulty discarding possessions, Severe clutter of living spaces, Distress upon discarding

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MHS-028
Reason for Visit: Minor trauma (fall) and severe acute agitation during an attempted forced clean-up by family members.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and adult child
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a fall over accumulated debris resulting in a painful, non-weight-bearing knee, combined with extreme emotional distress, hyperventilation, and hostility toward the family member attempting to clear the hazard. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to assess the traumatic injury, rule out fracture, and de-escalate a highly volatile behavioral crisis within an unsafe environment.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I tripped over my important papers because he was trying to throw them away! My knee is killing me, and I want him out of my house!"
History of Present Illness: The patient reports an acute injury (right knee pain) following a trip and fall over a stack of magazines in the hallway. The adult child reports the patient has a chronic, persistent difficulty discarding or parting with possessions, regardless of their actual value (e.g., newspapers from 1990). The patient expresses a strong perceived need to save the items and exhibits profound distress associated with discarding them. There is a massive accumulation of possessions that congest and clutter active living areas, leading to a substantial compromise of the intended use of living spaces (e.g., the kitchen cannot be used for cooking). The clutter poses compromised physical safety within the home environment, directly causing today's fall. The patient displays extremely poor insight regarding the severity of the clutter, claiming it is "organized." There is significant social isolation stemming from embarrassment, though the patient denies this.
Functional Status: Currently unable to bear weight on the right leg; highly limited mobility even at baseline due to environmental hazards.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extreme fire and fall hazards; severe interpersonal conflict with family.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 150/95 | HR: 105 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Highly agitated, defensive, sitting on a chair surrounded by debris, clutching the right knee.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals reactive tachycardia. Musculoskeletal exam of the right knee reveals mild diffuse swelling and tenderness over the medial collateral ligament. No bony point tenderness over the patella or fibular head. Range of motion is limited by pain but structurally intact. Ottawa knee rules negative for mandatory X-ray. Mental state examination is dominated by severe distress upon discarding, absolute difficulty discarding possessions, and defensiveness regarding the severe clutter of living spaces.
Environmental Observations: Narrow "goat trails" are the only navigable paths through the house. Piles of combustible material (paper, clothing) reach the ceiling in some rooms.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient cannot locate their regular antihypertensive medications amid the clutter.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Hoarding Disorder - Status: Exacerbated (Acute injury and behavioral crisis)
Differentials:

Acute Fracture (Patella/Femur) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of bony tenderness, intact extensor mechanism, and negative Ottawa rules, indicating a soft tissue sprain.

Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder - Hoarding is now a distinct diagnosis; the behavior is not driven by intrusive, unwanted obsessions, but rather by a perceived value of the items and distress at their loss.

Major Neurocognitive Disorder (Dementia) - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary cause, as the hoarding behavior has been lifelong and the patient is fully oriented with intact short-term memory, though executive dysfunction is present.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Extremely High (Fire and fall risk)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient experienced a traumatic injury secondary to a severely compromised living environment, compounded by an acute behavioral crisis. Telephone triage appropriately identified the inability to bear weight and the high level of hostility. Urgent after-hours medical assessment was mandatory to physically examine the knee to rule out a fracture, provide acute analgesia, and mediate the immediate conflict between the patient and the family member to prevent further violence or panic. Delaying care would have left an undiagnosed joint injury in an environment where mobility is already critically dangerous.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Right knee wrapped with a compression bandage. Administered oral Ibuprofen 400mg for acute analgesia. Advised rest, ice, compression, and elevation (RICE). Expected investigation results (outpatient): standardized home environment assessments mapping clutter and safety hazards.
Positive Behavioral Change: Implement "Safety Corridor Agreement." Negotiate the clearing of just one specific 3-foot pathway from the bed to the bathroom. Frame this not as "throwing things away," but as "moving items to ensure the knee can heal." Reward this small environmental modification with a cessation of the family's broader clean-up efforts for the weekend. This operant conditioning uses the reduction of interpersonal conflict to reinforce the creation of basic physical safety.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to Adult Protective Services (due to self-neglect/hazard) and a specialized hoarding task force.
DME/Safety (Optional): Compression bandage provided. Walker contraindicated due to clutter.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 days for knee reassessment and mental health care plan initiation.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient becomes completely unable to bear weight, if the knee swells massively, or if a fire/structural collapse occurs.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Acute musculoskeletal injury managed; the environmental hazards pose a severe, ongoing threat to life.
Clinical Course: The knee sprain will heal over 2-3 weeks, but the risk of recurrent falls remains near 100% unless the environment is modified.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If the pain in your knee becomes unbearable, or if you lose feeling in your foot, seek medical care. You must maintain a clear pathway to the exit in case of an emergency.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: You have sprained a ligament in your knee. It requires rest and a clear space to walk so you don't twist it again. The immediate goal is creating a safe walking path, not clearing the whole house today.
Actionable Steps: Keep the compression bandage on during the day. Rest the leg. You must agree to let your child clear a single pathway to the bathroom tonight to prevent another fall.

